WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.140
SEVERANCE:
Tech companies have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:06.780
laid off tens of thousands of employees but&nbsp;
they’ve also handed out hundreds of thousands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:12.960
of dollars worth of severance to each and every&nbsp;
employee that was laid off. Meta offered 16 weeks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:18.720
of base pay plus another 2 weeks of base pay for&nbsp;
every year of tenure. Google offered an identical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:23.940
package but some companies even took it to the&nbsp;
next level. Salesforce, for example, offered a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:31.320
minimum of 5 months worth of base pay and that’s&nbsp;
just base pay. Most severance packages also came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:35.580
along with stock vesting through the current&nbsp;
quarter or more, 6 months of health insurance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:41.700
the opportunity to cash out PTO, and compensation&nbsp;
for the 60 day notification period. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:46.560
most of the people who were laid off from big&nbsp;
tech walked away with nearly 6 months worth of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:52.620
full compensation. This is no doubt how severance&nbsp;
should be handled if layoffs are required but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:57.900
were these layoffs even worth it? I mean, these&nbsp;
companies are basically committing to pay these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:04.260
employees for the next 6 months. A director for&nbsp;
example would walk away with $400 to $500 grand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:10.020
Not to mention, they never really stopped hiring&nbsp;
either. Yes, we always see headlines about Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:16.920
or Meta implementing a scary indefinite hiring&nbsp;
freeze but this isn’t actually true. While hiring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:24.000
is definitely slower and more selective, Google&nbsp;
literally has over 1900 job openings posted as we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:29.160
speak. So, if these companies are just hiring back&nbsp;
new people as they layoff their existing staff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:36.120
where exactly is the cost savings? Sure, they can&nbsp;
pay the new people a marginally lower salary of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:43.080
10 or 20%. But, for a company like Google or&nbsp;
Microsoft, saving 10 or 20% on 10 or 20,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:49.440
employees is a rounding error. Not to mention&nbsp;
all of the terrible PR. I mean just look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:54.780
Apple. They’re coming off as some sort of heroes&nbsp;
for not doing layoffs while Meta is presented as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:59.700
suffering from Typhoid fever or something. So,&nbsp;
join me as we figure out just how much these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:04.680
companies are actually saving from layoffs and&nbsp;
the real reason they’re resorting to layoffs.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:11.220
COST SAVINGS DEBUNKED:
Let’s start off by taking a look at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:17.160
supposed benefit of layoffs: reduced operational&nbsp;
expenses. Given how much these companies pay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:22.800
the apparent savings are actually quite large.&nbsp;
Google for instance has a median compensation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:30.660
$295,000. Facebook comes in just under that&nbsp;
at $292,000. These are already big numbers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:36.660
but that’s just direct compensation of base&nbsp;
salary, stock, and bonus. These companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:41.940
also hand out extremely generous insurance&nbsp;
plans, 401k plans, retirement plans, free food,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:46.020
smartphones, monitors, phone plans, software&nbsp;
subscriptions, airplane tickets, hotel rooms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:50.700
and office space. They also have to pay social&nbsp;
security and medicare tax on these salaries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:57.960
so the indirect compensation itself easily adds&nbsp;
up to another $50 to 100k. Let’s call it $100k&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:02.580
to give these companies the benefit of the doubt.&nbsp;
This means that the average employee is costing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:09.960
Google and Facebook roughly $400,000. So, how much&nbsp;
exactly are the layoffs saving? Well, Google has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:15.660
thus far laid off 12,000 people and Facebook&nbsp;
has laid off 21,000 people. At $400k a pop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:21.660
Google is saving $4.8 billion per year and&nbsp;
Facebook is saving $8.4 billion per year. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:27.240
was the first time that I started thinking that&nbsp;
maybe the layoffs were worth it. I mean, we’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:33.600
talking about billions of dollars every single&nbsp;
year. That is until I faintly recalled that not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:39.540
a single one of these companies are losing money.&nbsp;
In fact, they’re not even remotely close to losing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:44.700
money. Some of them are straight up posting record&nbsp;
profits. And when you contextualize these savings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:49.980
with how much these companies are pulling in,&nbsp;
it just becomes even worse. In terms of revenue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:55.440
Google pulls in just under $300 billion per year&nbsp;
and Facebook pulls in just over $100 billion per&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.440 --> 00:04:01.260
year. So, the savings only equate to 6 days worth&nbsp;
of revenue for Google and 26 days worth of revenue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:07.680
for Facebook. Obviously, they haven’t hit the&nbsp;
jackpot, but with that being said, savings are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:14.280
savings. Why make less money when you can just&nbsp;
make more money which brings us back to the idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:20.040
of maybe the tech companies were right for doing&nbsp;
layoffs until you consider natural attrition.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:26.160
Here’s a secret. These big tech companies are&nbsp;
actually really bad at employee retention. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:31.860
it’s not because of bad pay or bad work culture&nbsp;
maybe with the exception of Amazon. It’s actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:36.660
because most FAANG level tech bros are simply&nbsp;
not dependent on the company. A lot of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:41.700
have enough money to retire by the time their 30,&nbsp;
so it’s not like they’re held back by their job.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:47.880
As such, they’re constantly looking for better&nbsp;
opportunities and leave as soon as they find one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:53.160
This is why the average tenure at most tech&nbsp;
companies is only 2 years. For Google, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:59.520
corresponds to an employee turnover rate of 13%.&nbsp;
Let me put that in another way. Google has to hire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:06.060
25,000 people every year just to maintain&nbsp;
their headcount. Meta has to hire 11,000,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:13.140
Microsoft has to hire 28,000, and Apple has to&nbsp;
hire 21,000. And all of that is with just a 13%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:18.720
turnover rate. Some of these companies have&nbsp;
turnovers that’s are way worse which raises a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:23.640
rather interesting question: could these companies&nbsp;
have just stopped hiring and waited it out?

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:26.500
ALTERNATIVES TO LAYOFFS:&nbsp;

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:32.940
Well, let’s just say, the minute that the first&nbsp;
big tech company announced mass layoffs, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:38.520
strategy became a lot harder. Suddenly, people are&nbsp;
scared to join that new startup or even switch to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:44.100
another big tech company. In fact, I bet natural&nbsp;
attrition is the lowest its been since like 2008,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:49.800
and it’s all thanks to Meta who kicked off the&nbsp;
big tech layoff season back in November. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:55.680
this isn’t to say that this strategy became&nbsp;
impossible. I mean, Apple and Nvidia are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.680 --> 00:06:00.840
leveraging this strategy as we speak. But, if all&nbsp;
the big tech companies weathered out the storm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:06.120
together, the strategy would’ve simply been even&nbsp;
more effective. In fact, it would’ve negated the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:10.740
need for layoffs altogether for many companies.&nbsp;
Take Google for instance. They have to hire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:17.220
25,000 people every year just to keep up with&nbsp;
turnover. This means that instead of laying of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:22.200
12,000 people and giving them ridiculous severance&nbsp;
for 6 months, Google could have just implemented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:27.240
a real hiring freeze for 6 months and they&nbsp;
would’ve ended up with the same head count and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:33.780
the same cost savings. This idea is simply even&nbsp;
truer at Microsoft. So far, they’ve laid off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:40.140
10,000 people but they could’ve just frozen&nbsp;
hiring for 4.5 months. In fact, for Microsoft,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:46.380
it might’ve been cheaper to freeze hiring than&nbsp;
to do layoffs. And don’t even get me started on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:52.200
Amazon. Their attrition is so dang high that they&nbsp;
straight up measure it on a weekly basis. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:57.900
lose 3% of their warehouse workers every single&nbsp;
week, and to be honest, I don’t think their tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.900 --> 00:07:03.420
turnover is all that much better. The reality&nbsp;
is that for most companies, freezing hiring for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:08.820
6 months would’ve accomplished the same goal as&nbsp;
layoffs all with way better PR. The only notable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.820 --> 00:07:14.220
exception to this would be Meta who would have to&nbsp;
freeze hiring for 2 years which just goes to show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:21.180
how much they overhired. But for everyone else,&nbsp;
the situation wasn’t that bad. Also, freezing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:26.640
hiring isn’t the only alternative. There’s plenty&nbsp;
of other solutions like just cutting bonuses. Out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:33.360
of the average Googlers $300,000 salary, about&nbsp;
$30,000 is just a bonus. So, if Google had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:40.560
simply cut the bonus of all 190,000 employees,&nbsp;
that itself would’ve saved $5.7 billion or $900&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:46.440
million more than the layoffs. Also, that’s not&nbsp;
even considering that directors, and VPs, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:52.740
executives have exponentially larger bonuses. But,&nbsp;
Google didn’t even reduce bonuses, they simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:57.540
delayed them. Another solution would’ve been to&nbsp;
offer early retirement and voluntary resignations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.540 --> 00:08:02.580
This is usually what Southwest Airlines leans into&nbsp;
whenever they’re going through a turbulent period&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:09.000
like the pandemic. And news flash, Southwest has&nbsp;
never had layoffs despite having low single digit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:14.820
profit margins. So, if Southwest can get through&nbsp;
without layoffs, tech companies definitely can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.820 --> 00:08:19.860
with their 30-40% net margins. The only argument&nbsp;
against these alternative solutions would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:24.540
that companies give up control as to who&nbsp;
leaves. With layoffs, companies can slash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:30.180
certain departments and titles. But, this can&nbsp;
simply be corrected for with strategic hiring.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:36.300
If you want to get rid of recruiters, just stop&nbsp;
hiring recruiters. With time, things will level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.300 --> 00:08:41.520
out how you want. But then, if the savings from&nbsp;
layoffs are marginal at best and there’s little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:46.320
strategic benefit to layoffs, what’s the&nbsp;
real reason that everyone is doing layoffs?

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:47.820
THE REAL REASON:&nbsp;

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:55.380
To get to the root of why companies are doing&nbsp;
layoffs, we have to first identify who exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.380 --> 00:09:02.820
wants layoffs. It’s obviously not the employees&nbsp;
but it’s also the employers either. Do you really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:08.520
think these companies want to be the bad guy? They&nbsp;
already have a bunch of bad rep regarding invasion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.520 --> 00:09:14.220
of privacy, data collection, and monopolization.&nbsp;
Reputation wise, one of the only factors that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:19.860
are going in these companies’ favor nowadays is&nbsp;
that they pay well and have happy employees. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.860 --> 00:09:25.800
it makes no sense to screw that up as well. Not&nbsp;
to mention all of the damage layoffs cause to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.800 --> 00:09:32.100
company morale and ongoing projects. Tech CEOs&nbsp;
more than understand this and that’s why most of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:38.700
them don’t want to do layoffs. Take Tim Cook for&nbsp;
example. He’s taken a pay cut from $265 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.700 --> 00:09:46.260
in 2020 to $49 million this year. Similarly,&nbsp;
Zoom CEO, Eric Yuan has taken a 98% paycut. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.260 --> 00:09:51.720
I’m sure it’s hard to sympathize with these&nbsp;
billionaire CEOs. Oh no, Tim is only making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:57.660
$50 million this year boo hoo, but bear with me.&nbsp;
The point is that these CEOs are doing their best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.660 --> 00:10:02.640
to prevent layoffs themselves. Hold on wait a&nbsp;
minute. What do I mean they’re trying to prevent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:07.740
layoffs. They’re literally the ones approving&nbsp;
them. Well, as far as the public is concerned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:12.840
that’s true. The CEOs are the ones who&nbsp;
take all the blame and bad rep for layoffs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.840 --> 00:10:19.080
and they’re definitely by no means saints,&nbsp;
but they’re not the ones behind layoffs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.080 --> 00:10:25.740
The ones who are really behind the layoffs are&nbsp;
people like this. You probably have no idea who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.740 --> 00:10:31.980
this guy is because people like this lurk in&nbsp;
the shadows but they pull all the strings. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.980 --> 00:10:37.860
is Christopher Hohn: the founder of TCI Fund&nbsp;
Management. He’s a multi billionaire with a $6&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:43.440
billion stake in Google. And since last November,&nbsp;
he has been constantly sending letters to Sundar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:48.240
and calling out Google amongst investor circles.&nbsp;
“We are writing to express our view that the cost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:54.720
base of Alphabet is too high and management needs&nbsp;
to take aggressive action.” “The company has too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.720 --> 00:11:00.060
many employees and the cost per employee is too&nbsp;
high.” Within hours of this letter being sent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.060 --> 00:11:06.960
Google shares were up 4.4% which doesn't sound&nbsp;
like a lot but for a company as big as Google,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.960 --> 00:11:13.620
we’re talking about a market cap increase of over&nbsp;
$50 billion making it crystal clear that this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.620 --> 00:11:19.080
what rich investors want. The reality is that&nbsp;
people like Christopher Hohn have no idea about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.080 --> 00:11:24.240
tech. They think it’s like some sort of factory&nbsp;
where you have to maximize output and minimize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:31.020
input. That’s not how tech works and tech&nbsp;
CEOs obviously know that but that’s completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.020 --> 00:11:36.480
irrelevant. You get a dozen Christopher Hohn’s&nbsp;
together and boom, it’s Sundar or Tim that’s out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.480 --> 00:11:43.320
of a job. So, naturally, they do as they’re asked&nbsp;
even though it’s not all that effective. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.320 --> 00:11:48.480
I don’t want to understate the aggressiveness of&nbsp;
these investors. After the first round of layoffs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:53.940
at Google, the first thing Chris did was send&nbsp;
in another letter asking for far more. He wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.940 --> 00:12:00.540
Google to cut a total of 40,000 jobs and reduce&nbsp;
pay substantially. And Chris is just one example.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.540 --> 00:12:08.040
Most big investors do the same thing just behind&nbsp;
closed doors. And that’s why everyone is suddenly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.040 --> 00:12:12.960
doing layoffs even though its effectiveness&nbsp;
is questionable to say the least. Something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:17.880
else that’s questionable is Goodwill’s&nbsp;
Goodwill. Check out this video to learn more.

